(self.CURRICULUM ||= { lessons: [] }).lessons.push(...[{"n": 1, "title": "Lesson 1: Basic Korean Sentences", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit1/unit-1-lessons-1-8/unit-1-lesson-1/", "vocab": [{"ko": "한국", "en": "Korea", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "도시", "en": "city", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "이름", "en": "name", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "저", "en": "I, me (formal)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "나", "en": "I, me (informal)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "남자", "en": "man", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "여자", "en": "woman", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "이", "en": "this (+ noun)", "pos": "other"}, {"ko": "그", "en": "that (+ noun, near you / just mentioned)", "pos": "other"}, {"ko": "저", "en": "that (+ noun, over there)", "pos": "other"}, {"ko": "것", "en": "thing", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "이것", "en": "this (thing)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "그것", "en": "that (thing)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "저것", "en": "that (thing over there)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "의자", "en": "chair", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "탁자", "en": "table", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "선생님", "en": "teacher (respectful, how you address them)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "침대", "en": "bed", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "집", "en": "house", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "차", "en": "car (everyday)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "사람", "en": "person", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "책", "en": "book", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "컴퓨터", "en": "computer", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "나무", "en": "tree/wood", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "소파", "en": "sofa", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "중국", "en": "China", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "일본", "en": "Japan", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "문", "en": "door", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "의사", "en": "doctor", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "학생", "en": "student", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "이다", "en": "to be", "pos": "other"}, {"ko": "네", "en": "yes", "pos": "other"}, {"ko": "아니", "en": "no (casual)", "pos": "other"}], "grammar": [{"point": "Sentence word order (S-O-V)", "explanation": "In English the verb comes in the middle: \"I eat an apple\". In Korean the predicate always comes last. The predicate is the verb, the adjective, or 이다 (to be). Everything else comes before it.\n\nThe usual order is subject → object → verb. Build it step by step: 저 (I) + 사과 (apple) + 먹어요 (eat) → 저는 사과를 먹어요. Literally \"I apple eat\".\n\nThe particles attached to each noun (는/은, 를/을) show who does what, so the nouns can sometimes move around. The verb still stays at the end. A subject that is obvious from context is often left out: 사과를 먹어요 can mean \"I eat an apple\".\n\nA common beginner mistake is to copy English order: 저는 먹어요 사과를 is wrong.", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 사과를 먹어요.", "en": "I eat an apple."}, {"ko": "선생님은 책을 읽어요.", "en": "The teacher reads a book."}, {"ko": "그 남자는 의사예요.", "en": "That man is a doctor."}, {"ko": "사과를 먹어요.", "en": "(I) eat an apple.", "note": "No subject: Korean drops it when it is obvious."}, {"ko": "그 여자는 문을 닫아요.", "en": "That woman closes the door."}]}, {"point": "~는/은 topic marker", "explanation": "는/은 marks the topic, which is what the sentence is about. Think of it as \"as for…\": 저는 학생이에요 is literally \"as for me, (I) am a student\". At this stage the topic is usually the subject.\n\nChoose by the last letter of the noun. If it ends in a vowel, add 는: 저 → 저는, 의자 → 의자는. If it ends in a consonant (it has a bottom letter), add 은: 집 → 집은, 선생님 → 선생님은. Both mean the same; the choice just makes it easier to say.\n\n저 (polite) and 나 (casual) both mean \"I\" and both end in a vowel: 저는, 나는. Use 저는 with anyone you don't know well.\n\n는/은 can also add a sense of contrast (\"I, unlike others…\"). You will see this more in later lessons.", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 학생이에요.", "en": "I am a student."}, {"ko": "선생님은 한국 사람이에요.", "en": "The teacher is Korean."}, {"ko": "이 집은 커요.", "en": "This house is big."}, {"ko": "나는 남자야.", "en": "I am a man."}, {"ko": "그 여자는 의사예요.", "en": "That woman is a doctor."}, {"ko": "이 침대는 작아요.", "en": "This bed is small."}]}, {"point": "~를/을 object marker", "explanation": "를/을 marks the object: the thing an action is done to. In 저는 책을 읽어요 (I read a book), the book is what gets read, so it takes 을.\n\nChoose by the last letter of the noun. If it ends in a vowel, add 를: 컴퓨터 → 컴퓨터를, 의자 → 의자를. If it ends in a consonant, add 을: 책 → 책을, 문 → 문을.\n\nThe object sits just before the verb: subject + 는/은, then object + 를/을, then the verb. Only action verbs take an object. 이다 and adjectives never do, so there is no 를/을 in 저는 학생이에요.\n\nIn casual speech the object marker is often dropped (책 읽어요), but keep it while you are learning.", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 책을 읽어요.", "en": "I read a book."}, {"ko": "학생은 컴퓨터를 원해요.", "en": "The student wants a computer."}, {"ko": "아버지는 의자를 만들어요.", "en": "Father makes a chair."}, {"ko": "남자는 문을 열어요.", "en": "The man opens the door."}, {"ko": "저는 소파를 원해요.", "en": "I want a sofa."}, {"ko": "선생님은 문을 닫아요.", "en": "The teacher closes the door."}]}, {"point": "이다 (to be) with nouns", "explanation": "이다 means \"to be\" in the sense \"A is B\", where B is a noun: I am a student, this is a chair. It attaches directly to the noun with no space.\n\nIn polite speech 이다 becomes 이에요 or 예요, depending on the noun's last letter. After a consonant use 이에요: 학생 → 학생이에요, 책 → 책이에요. After a vowel use 예요: 의자 → 의자예요, 의사 → 의사예요. Never say 의자이에요. In casual speech it is 이야 after a consonant and 야 after a vowel: 학생이야, 남자야.\n\nDon't use 이다 with adjectives. \"The house is big\" is 집은 커요, not 집은 커이에요, because adjectives already contain \"to be\".\n\nTo ask a question, keep the same form and raise your voice at the end: 학생이에요? (Are you a student?)", "examples": [{"ko": "그 여자는 학생이에요.", "en": "That woman is a student."}, {"ko": "이것은 의자예요.", "en": "This is a chair."}, {"ko": "그 사람은 선생님이에요.", "en": "That person is a teacher."}, {"ko": "그것은 제 차예요.", "en": "That is my car."}, {"ko": "선생님은 중국 사람이에요?", "en": "Is the teacher Chinese?", "note": "Same form as a statement; only the intonation rises."}, {"ko": "그 남자는 의사야.", "en": "That man is a doctor.", "note": "Casual: 의사 ends in a vowel, so 야."}]}, {"point": "이/그/저 + 것 (this/that/that over there)", "explanation": "Korean has three pointing words. 이 means \"this\" (near me). 그 means \"that\" (near you, or something we just mentioned). 저 means \"that over there\" (far from both of us).\n\nThey go straight before a noun, with a space: 이 책 (this book), 그 사람 (that person), 저 나무 (that tree over there).\n\nAdd 것 (thing) to get \"this one\" and \"that one\": 이 + 것 → 이것, 그 + 것 → 그것, 저 + 것 → 저것. These are written as one word and act like nouns, so they take particles. 것 ends in a consonant, so it is 이것은 and 그것을.\n\nCareful: 저 also means \"I\" (polite). 저는 학생이에요 means \"I am a student\", but 저 사람은 학생이에요 means \"That person over there is a student\". If a noun follows 저, it means \"that\".", "examples": [{"ko": "이것은 책이에요.", "en": "This is a book."}, {"ko": "그것은 컴퓨터예요.", "en": "That is a computer."}, {"ko": "저것은 나무예요.", "en": "That over there is a tree."}, {"ko": "저 사람은 의사예요.", "en": "That person over there is a doctor."}, {"ko": "이 차는 일본 차예요.", "en": "This car is a Japanese car."}, {"ko": "저것은 의자예요?", "en": "Is that over there a chair?"}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "이 남자___ 의사예요.", "answer": "는", "options": ["는", "은", "를", "을"], "en": "This man is a doctor.", "why": "남자 ends in a vowel, so the topic marker is 는."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "그 선생님___ 일본 사람이에요.", "answer": "은", "options": ["은", "는", "을", "를"], "en": "That teacher is Japanese.", "why": "선생님 ends in a consonant, so the topic marker is 은."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "이것은 침대___.", "answer": "예요", "options": ["예요", "이에요", "에요", "이예요"], "en": "This is a bed.", "why": "침대 ends in a vowel, so 이다 becomes 예요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "그 남자는 학생___.", "answer": "이에요", "options": ["이에요", "예요", "에요", "이예요"], "en": "That man is a student.", "why": "학생 ends in a consonant, so 이다 becomes 이에요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "여자는 문___ 열어요.", "answer": "을", "options": ["을", "를", "에", "의"], "en": "The woman opens the door.", "why": "문 is the object and ends in a consonant, so it takes 을."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 소파___ 원해요.", "answer": "를", "options": ["를", "을", "에", "의"], "en": "I want a sofa.", "why": "소파 is the object and ends in a vowel, so it takes 를."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "___ 사람은 의사예요.", "answer": "저", "options": ["저", "이것", "그것", "저것"], "en": "That person over there is a doctor.", "why": "Before a noun use 저 alone; 저것 already means 'that thing' and cannot take another noun."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "___은 의자예요.", "answer": "저것", "options": ["저것", "저", "이", "그"], "en": "That (over there) is a chair.", "why": "On their own, 이/그/저 need 것 to mean 'this/that thing'."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "That person is Japanese.", "ko": "그 사람은 일본 사람이에요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "This is a table.", "ko": "이것은 탁자예요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "This is a Korean car.", "ko": "이것은 한국 차예요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "The woman wants a car.", "ko": "여자는 차를 원해요."}], "reading": {"ko": "저는 민수예요. 저는 한국 사람이에요. 저는 학생이에요. 이 여자는 유키예요. 유키는 일본 사람이에요. 유키는 선생님이에요.", "en": "I am Minsu. I am Korean. I am a student. This woman is Yuki. Yuki is Japanese. Yuki is a teacher.", "questions": [{"q": "What is Minsu?", "a": "A student (학생)."}, {"q": "Where is Yuki from?", "a": "Japan (일본)."}, {"q": "What does Yuki do?", "a": "She is a teacher (선생님)."}]}}}, {"n": 2, "title": "Lesson 2: Korean Particles 이/가", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit1/unit-1-lessons-1-8/unit-1-lesson-2/", "vocab": [{"ko": "나라", "en": "country", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "가방", "en": "bag/backpack", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "창문", "en": "window", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "잡지", "en": "magazine", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "방", "en": "room", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "냉장고", "en": "refrigerator", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "개", "en": "dog", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "강아지", "en": "puppy", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "고양이", "en": "cat", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "쥐", "en": "rat, mouse", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "펜", "en": "pen", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "전화기", "en": "phone", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "커피", "en": "coffee", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "식당", "en": "restaurant", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "건물", "en": "building", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "텔레비전", "en": "television", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "미국", "en": "USA", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "캐나다", "en": "Canada", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "호텔", "en": "hotel", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "학교", "en": "school", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "은행", "en": "bank", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "안", "en": "inside (of a space)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "위", "en": "on top", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "밑", "en": "below", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "옆", "en": "beside", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "뒤", "en": "behind", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "앞", "en": "in front", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "여기", "en": "here", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "있다", "en": "to be at a location", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "있다", "en": "to have something", "pos": "verb"}], "grammar": [{"point": "~이/가 subject marker", "explanation": "이/가 marks the subject: the person or thing that does the action or that is being described. If the noun ends in a consonant, add 이: 책 → 책이. If it ends in a vowel, add 가: 고양이 → 고양이가, 개 → 개가.\n\nWith \"I\", the word changes shape: 저 + 가 → 제가, 나 + 가 → 내가. Never say 저가 or 나가.\n\nHow is it different from 는/은? 는/은 sets up what you are talking about (\"as for me…\"). 이/가 puts the focus on who or what. 제가 학생이에요 sounds like \"I'm the student (not someone else)\". Use 이/가 when something is mentioned for the first time: 고양이가 방에 있어요 (there's a cat in the room).\n\n이/가 is also used with 있다 and with adjectives describing a thing: 개가 커요.", "examples": [{"ko": "고양이가 방에 있어요.", "en": "The cat is in the room."}, {"ko": "책이 탁자 위에 있어요.", "en": "The book is on the table."}, {"ko": "제가 학생이에요.", "en": "I am the student."}, {"ko": "개가 커요.", "en": "The dog is big."}, {"ko": "내가 의사야.", "en": "I'm the doctor.", "note": "나 + 가 → 내가."}, {"ko": "방이 작아요.", "en": "The room is small."}]}, {"point": "있다 (to have)", "explanation": "있다 has two meanings: \"to be somewhere\" and \"to have\". For \"to have\", Korean says that the thing exists for the person: Person는/은 + Thing이/가 + 있다.\n\nStep by step: 저 + 는 → 저는; 가방 + 이 → 가방이; 있다 → 있어요. Result: 저는 가방이 있어요 (I have a bag).\n\nThe common mistake is to use 를/을 because \"have\" takes an object in English. 저는 가방을 있어요 is wrong. The thing you have takes 이/가.\n\nTo ask \"Do you have…?\", just ask a question: 펜이 있어요? The casual form is 있어: 나는 강아지가 있어.", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 가방이 있어요.", "en": "I have a bag."}, {"ko": "선생님은 차가 있어요.", "en": "The teacher has a car."}, {"ko": "나는 강아지가 있어.", "en": "I have a puppy."}, {"ko": "제 친구는 고양이가 있어요.", "en": "My friend has a cat."}, {"ko": "펜이 있어요?", "en": "Do you have a pen?"}, {"ko": "그 호텔은 식당이 있어요.", "en": "That hotel has a restaurant."}]}, {"point": "~에 (place or time)", "explanation": "~에 is a small particle that points to a place or a time. It attaches directly to the noun and is the same after a vowel or a consonant: 학교에, 집에, 방에.\n\nWith 있다 it says where something is: 저는 학교에 있어요 (I'm at school). With movement verbs like 가다 and 오다 it says where someone is going: 은행에 가요 (I go to the bank). With time words it means \"at\" or \"on\": 월요일에 (on Monday).\n\nSome time words never take 에. 오늘, 어제, 내일 and 지금 are used on their own: say 내일 가요, not 내일에 가요.\n\n~에 marks where something is or where it goes, not where an action takes place. \"I eat at a restaurant\" uses a different particle (에서), which comes later.", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 학교에 있어요.", "en": "I am at school."}, {"ko": "우유는 냉장고에 있어요.", "en": "The milk is in the fridge."}, {"ko": "저는 은행에 가요.", "en": "I go to the bank."}, {"ko": "고양이는 방에 있어요.", "en": "The cat is in the room."}, {"ko": "우리는 식당에 가요.", "en": "We go to the restaurant."}, {"ko": "저는 월요일에 은행에 가요.", "en": "I go to the bank on Monday.", "note": "Two 에s: one for the time, one for the place."}]}, {"point": "Position words + ~에 있다 (in/on/under/beside/behind/in front)", "explanation": "Where English says \"on the table\", Korean says \"at the table's top\". The pattern is: reference noun + position word + 에 있다.\n\nThe position words are nouns: 안 (inside), 위 (top), 밑 (under), 옆 (beside), 뒤 (behind), 앞 (in front). Build it in order: 탁자 (table) → 탁자 위 (table top) → 탁자 위에 (at the table top) → 탁자 위에 있어요 (is on the table).\n\nThe order is the reverse of English: \"under the chair\" is 의자 밑에, \"in the bag\" is 가방 안에. The thing you are locating comes first with 는/은 or 이/가: 펜은 탁자 위에 있어요.\n\n여기 (here) works on its own without a position word: 여기에 있어요 (it's here).", "examples": [{"ko": "펜은 탁자 위에 있어요.", "en": "The pen is on the table."}, {"ko": "개는 의자 밑에 있어요.", "en": "The dog is under the chair."}, {"ko": "은행은 호텔 옆에 있어요.", "en": "The bank is next to the hotel."}, {"ko": "식당은 학교 앞에 있어요.", "en": "The restaurant is in front of the school."}, {"ko": "전화기는 가방 안에 있어요.", "en": "The phone is in the bag."}, {"ko": "고양이가 소파 뒤에 있어요.", "en": "There is a cat behind the sofa."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "고양이___ 소파 위에 있어요.", "answer": "가", "options": ["가", "이", "를", "에"], "en": "The cat is on the sofa.", "why": "고양이 ends in a vowel, so the subject marker is 가."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "가방___ 의자 밑에 있어요.", "answer": "이", "options": ["이", "가", "을", "에"], "en": "The bag is under the chair.", "why": "가방 ends in a consonant, so the subject marker is 이."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "___ 선생님이에요.", "answer": "제가", "options": ["제가", "저가", "저이", "저를"], "en": "I am the teacher.", "why": "저 + 가 always becomes 제가."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 강아지___ 있어요.", "answer": "가", "options": ["가", "를", "이", "에"], "en": "I have a puppy.", "why": "With 있다 meaning 'have', the thing you have takes 이/가, not 을/를."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "민수는 펜___ 있어요.", "answer": "이", "options": ["이", "가", "을", "를"], "en": "Minsu has a pen.", "why": "The owned thing takes 이/가, and 펜 ends in a consonant, so 이."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "커피는 냉장고___ 있어요.", "answer": "에", "options": ["에", "을", "를", "의"], "en": "The coffee is in the fridge.", "why": "에 marks the place where something is."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "식당은 은행 ___에 있어요.", "answer": "뒤", "options": ["뒤", "앞", "옆", "안"], "en": "The restaurant is behind the bank.", "why": "뒤 means 'behind'; the reference noun comes first, then the position word."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "개는 탁자 밑___ 있어요.", "answer": "에", "options": ["에", "이", "를", "은"], "en": "The dog is under the table.", "why": "Position words take 에 before 있다: 밑에 있어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "The school is in front of the bank.", "ko": "학교는 은행 앞에 있어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I have a cat.", "ko": "저는 고양이가 있어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "The phone is in the bag.", "ko": "전화기는 가방 안에 있어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "There is a television in the room.", "ko": "방에 텔레비전이 있어요."}], "reading": {"ko": "민수는 호텔에 있어요. 호텔 옆에 식당이 있어요. 식당 앞에 은행이 있어요. 민수는 가방이 있어요. 가방 안에 책이 있어요. 책 위에 펜이 있어요.", "en": "Minsu is at the hotel. There is a restaurant next to the hotel. There is a bank in front of the restaurant. Minsu has a bag. There is a book in the bag. There is a pen on the book.", "questions": [{"q": "Where is Minsu?", "a": "At the hotel (호텔)."}, {"q": "What is next to the hotel?", "a": "A restaurant (식당)."}, {"q": "What is in Minsu's bag?", "a": "A book, with a pen on top of it."}]}}}, {"n": 3, "title": "Lesson 3: Korean Verbs/Adjectives", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit1/unit-1-lessons-1-8/unit-1-lesson-3/", "vocab": [{"ko": "음식", "en": "food", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "케이크", "en": "cake", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "공항", "en": "airport", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "공원", "en": "park", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "한국어", "en": "Korean (language)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "머리", "en": "head (also hair)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "다리", "en": "leg", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "손가락", "en": "finger", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "귀", "en": "ear", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "팔", "en": "arm", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "눈", "en": "eye", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "입", "en": "mouth, lips", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "배", "en": "stomach", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "버스", "en": "bus", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "배", "en": "boat", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "우리", "en": "us/we", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "먹다", "en": "to eat", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "가다", "en": "to go", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "만나다", "en": "to meet", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "닫다", "en": "to close", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "열다", "en": "to open", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "원하다", "en": "to want (an object)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "만들다", "en": "to make", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "하다", "en": "to do", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "말하다", "en": "to speak", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "이해하다", "en": "to understand", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "좋아하다", "en": "to like", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "크다", "en": "to be big", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "작다", "en": "to be small", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "새롭다", "en": "to be new", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "낡다", "en": "to be old (worn out)", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "비싸다", "en": "to be expensive", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "싸다", "en": "to be cheap", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "아름답다", "en": "to be beautiful", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "뚱뚱하다", "en": "to be fat, chubby", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "길다", "en": "to be long", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "좋다", "en": "to be good", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "아주", "en": "very (everyday)", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "매우", "en": "very (written/formal)", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "너무", "en": "too, very", "pos": "adverb"}], "grammar": [{"point": "Verbs: dictionary form ~다 and the stem", "explanation": "Every Korean verb has a dictionary form ending in 다: 먹다 (to eat), 가다 (to go), 하다 (to do). This is what you find in a dictionary, but you rarely say it like that.\n\nTake off 다 and you get the stem: 먹다 → 먹, 가다 → 가, 공부하다 → 공부하. All endings for tense and politeness attach to the stem. For example, the polite present ending gives 먹 + 어요 → 먹어요, 가 + 아요 → 가요, 하 → 해요. Lessons 5 and 6 explain how to pick the ending; for now, learn to spot the stem.\n\nMany verbs are a noun + 하다: 이해 + 하다 → 이해하다 (to understand), 말 + 하다 → 말하다 (to speak). They all conjugate the same way, ending in 해요.\n\nThe verb goes last. If it acts on something, that object takes 을/를: 저는 음식을 먹어요.", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 음식을 먹어요.", "en": "I eat food."}, {"ko": "저는 문을 닫아요.", "en": "I close the door."}, {"ko": "선생님은 한국어를 해요.", "en": "The teacher speaks Korean.", "note": "With a language, 하다 is more natural than 말하다: 한국어를 해요."}, {"ko": "우리는 공원에 가요.", "en": "We go to the park."}, {"ko": "저는 한국어를 이해해요.", "en": "I understand Korean.", "note": "이해하다 → 이해하 → 이해해요."}, {"ko": "저는 케이크를 만들어요.", "en": "I make a cake."}]}, {"point": "Adjectives (descriptive verbs)", "explanation": "In Korean, adjectives are a kind of verb. 크다 doesn't mean \"big\" but \"to be big\". So 이 버스는 커요 is a full sentence: \"This bus is big\". No 이다 is needed.\n\nThey conjugate just like verbs: drop 다 to get the stem, then add the ending. 작다 → 작 + 아요 → 작아요; 싸다 → 싸 + 아요 → 싸요; 뚱뚱하다 → 뚱뚱해요. Some change in special ways (크다 → 커요, 아름답다 → 아름다워요); Lesson 7 explains these.\n\nBecause they describe rather than act, adjectives never take an object with 을/를. The thing being described is the subject, marked with 는/은 or 이/가.\n\nA common mistake is to add 이에요: 버스는 크이에요 is wrong. Just say 버스는 커요.", "examples": [{"ko": "이 버스는 커요.", "en": "This bus is big."}, {"ko": "그 케이크는 비싸요.", "en": "That cake is expensive."}, {"ko": "공원은 아주 작아요.", "en": "The park is very small."}, {"ko": "그 여자는 아름다워요.", "en": "That woman is beautiful."}, {"ko": "이 컴퓨터는 싸요.", "en": "This computer is cheap.", "note": "싸 + 아요 → 싸요 (the two ㅏ sounds merge)."}, {"ko": "그 강아지는 뚱뚱해요.", "en": "That puppy is chubby."}]}, {"point": "~의 possessive particle", "explanation": "의 shows ownership, like English \"'s\". \"A의 B\" means \"A's B\": 선생님의 차 (the teacher's car). It is the same after a vowel or a consonant. When it means \"'s\", 의 is usually pronounced like 에.\n\nWith \"I\", the words usually shorten: 저 + 의 → 제 (my, polite), 나 + 의 → 내 (my, casual). So 제 친구 means \"my friend\". 제 and 내 are far more common than 저의 and 나의.\n\nIn speech, 의 is often left out when the meaning is clear: 선생님 차, 친구 집. You will still see it in writing.\n\nThe owner always comes first, which is the opposite of English \"of\": \"the eyes of that woman\" is 그 여자의 눈.", "examples": [{"ko": "선생님의 차는 커요.", "en": "The teacher's car is big."}, {"ko": "제 친구는 의사예요.", "en": "My friend is a doctor."}, {"ko": "그 여자의 눈은 아름다워요.", "en": "That woman's eyes are beautiful."}, {"ko": "내 손가락은 길어.", "en": "My fingers are long."}, {"ko": "이것은 제 가방이에요.", "en": "This is my bag."}, {"ko": "그 남자의 집은 작아요.", "en": "That man's house is small."}]}, {"point": "좋다 vs 좋아하다", "explanation": "These two look alike but work differently. 좋다 is an adjective meaning \"to be good\". 좋아하다 is a verb meaning \"to like\".\n\n좋다 describes the subject, so there is no object: 이 책은 좋아요 (This book is good). Conjugation: 좋 + 아요 → 좋아요.\n\n좋아하다 is something a person does to a thing, so the thing liked takes 을/를: 저는 케이크를 좋아해요 (I like cake). Conjugation: 좋아하 → 좋아해요.\n\nThe common mistake is mixing them: 저는 케이크를 좋아요 is wrong. If the sentence has 을/를, you need 좋아해요. Later you will also see 케이크가 좋아요 for \"I like cake\"; with 좋다 the thing keeps 이/가.", "examples": [{"ko": "이 책은 좋아요.", "en": "This book is good.", "note": "For food, 맛있어요 (it tastes good) is more natural than 좋아요."}, {"ko": "저는 케이크를 좋아해요.", "en": "I like cake."}, {"ko": "이 의자는 좋아요.", "en": "This chair is good."}, {"ko": "우리는 한국 음식을 좋아해요.", "en": "We like Korean food."}, {"ko": "이 컴퓨터는 아주 좋아요.", "en": "This computer is very good."}, {"ko": "한국 음식을 좋아해요?", "en": "Do you like Korean food?"}]}, {"point": "우리 (we, us, our)", "explanation": "우리 means \"we\" or \"us\". With particles: 우리는 (we, topic), 우리가 (we, subject), 우리를 (us, object).\n\nPut 우리 directly before a noun and it means \"our\": 우리 집 (our house), 우리 선생님 (our teacher). You don't need 의 here; 우리의 is mostly written.\n\nKoreans say 우리 where English says \"my\" for things shared with a group: family, home, school, country. 우리 어머니 means \"my mother\", even for an only child. For personal things like a bag or phone, use 제/내: 제 가방.\n\n저희 is a humble version of 우리, used when you need to be very polite to the listener. You will hear it in shops and offices.", "examples": [{"ko": "우리는 학생이에요.", "en": "We are students."}, {"ko": "우리 집은 작아요.", "en": "Our house is small."}, {"ko": "우리 선생님은 한국 사람이에요.", "en": "Our teacher is Korean."}, {"ko": "우리 집은 커요.", "en": "Our house is big.", "note": "우리 also means \"our\": 우리 집."}, {"ko": "우리 어머니는 선생님이에요.", "en": "My mother is a teacher.", "note": "Koreans say \"our mother\" even when English says \"my\"."}, {"ko": "선생님은 우리를 이해해요.", "en": "The teacher understands us."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 케이크___ 좋아해요.", "answer": "를", "options": ["를", "가", "을", "의"], "en": "I like cake.", "why": "좋아하다 is a verb that takes an object, and 케이크 ends in a vowel, so 를."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "이 케이크는 ___.", "answer": "좋아요", "options": ["좋아요", "좋아해요", "좋아예요", "좋이에요"], "en": "This cake is good.", "why": "좋다 is an adjective meaning 'to be good'; 좋아하다 means 'to like'."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "제 친구는 일본 음식을 ___.", "answer": "좋아해요", "options": ["좋아해요", "좋아요", "좋아요해요", "좋다예요"], "en": "My friend likes Japanese food.", "why": "An object with 을/를 needs the verb 좋아하다, not the adjective 좋다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "선생님___ 가방은 비싸요.", "answer": "의", "options": ["의", "에", "를", "가"], "en": "The teacher's bag is expensive.", "why": "의 between two nouns makes the first one the owner."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "___ 선생님은 아주 좋아요.", "answer": "우리", "options": ["우리", "우리는", "우리를", "저는"], "en": "Our teacher is very good.", "why": "Before a noun, 우리 means 'our' and needs no particle."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "이 버스는 아주 ___.", "answer": "커요", "options": ["커요", "크다예요", "크이에요", "크예요"], "en": "This bus is very big.", "why": "Adjectives already mean 'to be', so no 이에요; 크다 becomes 커요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 문___ 닫아요.", "answer": "을", "options": ["을", "를", "이", "에"], "en": "I close the door.", "why": "문 is the object of 닫다 and ends in a consonant, so 을."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "우리는 공항___ 가요.", "answer": "에", "options": ["에", "의", "이", "가"], "en": "We go to the airport.", "why": "에 marks the destination of 가다."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I like coffee.", "ko": "저는 커피를 좋아해요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "That bag is expensive.", "ko": "그 가방은 비싸요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "We go to the hospital.", "ko": "우리는 병원에 가요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "My friend's house is small.", "ko": "제 친구의 집은 작아요."}], "reading": {"ko": "저는 민수예요. 우리 집은 공원 옆에 있어요. 우리 집은 작아요. 저는 한국 음식을 좋아해요. 우리는 식당에 가요. 그 식당의 음식은 아주 좋아요.", "en": "I am Minsu. Our house is next to the park. Our house is small. I like Korean food. We go to a restaurant. That restaurant's food is very good.", "questions": [{"q": "Where is Minsu's house?", "a": "Next to the park (공원)."}, {"q": "What food does Minsu like?", "a": "Korean food (한국 음식)."}, {"q": "How is the restaurant's food?", "a": "Very good."}]}}}, {"n": 4, "title": "Lesson 4: Korean Adjectives ~ㄴ/은", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit1/unit-1-lessons-1-8/unit-1-lesson-4/", "vocab": [{"ko": "길", "en": "street", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "거리", "en": "street/road", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "손", "en": "hand", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "영어", "en": "English", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "택시", "en": "taxi", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "열차", "en": "train", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "역", "en": "train/subway station", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "버스 정류장", "en": "bus stop", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "비행기", "en": "airplane", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "자전거", "en": "bicycle", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "아내", "en": "wife", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "아이", "en": "child", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "아들", "en": "son", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "딸", "en": "daughter", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "남편", "en": "husband", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "아버지", "en": "father", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "어머니", "en": "mother", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "편지", "en": "letter", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "맛", "en": "taste", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "식사", "en": "meal", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "아침", "en": "morning (also breakfast)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "아침식사", "en": "breakfast", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "물", "en": "water", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "사과", "en": "apple", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "돈", "en": "money", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "오다", "en": "to come", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "끝내다", "en": "to finish", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "춤추다", "en": "to dance", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "알다", "en": "to know", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "걷다", "en": "to walk", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "배우다", "en": "to learn", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "연습하다", "en": "to practice", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "생각하다", "en": "to think", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "살다", "en": "to live", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "끝나다", "en": "to be finished", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "위험하다", "en": "to be dangerous", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "잘생기다", "en": "to be handsome", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "못생기다", "en": "to be ugly", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "피곤하다", "en": "to be tired", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "다르다", "en": "to be different", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "슬프다", "en": "to be sad", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "맛있다", "en": "to be delicious", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "재미있다", "en": "to be fun, funny", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "많다", "en": "to be many, a lot", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "행복하다", "en": "to be happy", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "거기", "en": "there", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "저기", "en": "over there", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "지금", "en": "now (at this moment)", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "하지만", "en": "but", "pos": "other"}], "grammar": [{"point": "Adjective + ~ㄴ/은 to describe a noun", "explanation": "In Lesson 3 adjectives sat at the end of the sentence (차가 커요). To put an adjective in front of a noun, as in \"a big car\", change its ending.\n\nIf the stem ends in a vowel, add ㄴ under it: 크다 → 크 + ㄴ → 큰 (큰 차); 비싸다 → 비싼; 피곤하다 → 피곤한. If it ends in a consonant, add 은: 작다 → 작은 (작은 집); 많다 → 많은. If it ends in ㄹ, drop the ㄹ and add ㄴ: 길다 → 긴. Adjectives ending in 있다 take 는: 맛있다 → 맛있는, 재미있다 → 재미있는.\n\nThe describing word goes right before its noun, and the whole group takes the particle: 큰 차를 원해요.\n\nCompare: 큰 차 is \"a big car\" (a phrase), while 차가 커요 is \"the car is big\" (a sentence).", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 큰 차를 원해요.", "en": "I want a big car."}, {"ko": "비싼 음식은 맛있어요.", "en": "Expensive food is delicious."}, {"ko": "행복한 여자는 작은 집에 살아요.", "en": "The happy woman lives in a small house."}, {"ko": "그 남자는 재미있는 사람이에요.", "en": "That man is a fun person."}, {"ko": "저는 다른 책을 원해요.", "en": "I want a different book.", "note": "다르다 → 다르 + ㄴ → 다른."}, {"ko": "긴 열차가 와요.", "en": "A long train is coming.", "note": "길다 drops its ㄹ: 길 → 기 + ㄴ → 긴."}]}, {"point": "많다 (to be many): 많은 + noun vs noun이/가 많다", "explanation": "많다 means \"to be many\" or \"to be a lot\". It is used in two ways.\n\nAs the predicate at the end: Noun이/가 많아요 means \"there are a lot of (noun)\". 길에 사람이 많아요 (there are many people on the street). Add a topic to say who has a lot: 저는 돈이 많아요 (I have a lot of money). This is the most natural pattern.\n\nBefore a noun: 많다 → 많 + 은 → 많은 (\"a lot of\"): 많은 사람 (many people). This sounds a little more written. In speech, to say you do something a lot, Koreans usually use the adverb 많이 (Lesson 8): 많이 먹어요.\n\nThe ㅎ in 많 is silent before a vowel: 많아요 sounds like 마나요.", "examples": [{"ko": "길에 사람이 많아요.", "en": "There are many people on the street."}, {"ko": "저는 돈이 많아요.", "en": "I have a lot of money."}, {"ko": "많은 사람이 공원에 가요.", "en": "Many people go to the park."}, {"ko": "이 도시는 택시가 많아요.", "en": "This city has many taxis."}, {"ko": "공원에 나무가 많아요.", "en": "There are many trees in the park."}, {"ko": "많은 학생이 영어를 배워요.", "en": "Many students learn English."}]}, {"point": "~도 (also, too)", "explanation": "~도 means \"also\" or \"too\". It attaches straight to a noun and replaces 는/은, 이/가 or 을/를. Never stack them: 저는도 and 사과를도 are wrong. With 에 it is different: 에 stays and 도 goes after it (집에도, \"at home too\").\n\nStep by step: 저 + 는 → 저 + 도 → 저도 (I also). 사과 + 를 → 사과 + 도 → 사과도 (apples too). It is the same after a vowel or a consonant: 물도, 책도.\n\nThe meaning depends on which noun takes 도. 저도 사과를 먹어요 means \"I also eat apples\" (like someone else does). 저는 사과도 먹어요 means \"I eat apples too\" (as well as other things).\n\nOn its own, 저도요 is a handy reply: \"Me too.\"", "examples": [{"ko": "저도 학생이에요.", "en": "I am a student too."}, {"ko": "저는 사과도 먹어요.", "en": "I eat apples too."}, {"ko": "제 딸도 행복해요.", "en": "My daughter is happy too."}, {"ko": "아버지도 영어를 배워요.", "en": "Father also learns English."}, {"ko": "저도 사과를 먹어요.", "en": "I also eat apples.", "note": "Compare 저는 사과도 먹어요: here 도 is on \"I\", not on the apples."}, {"ko": "이 책도 재미있어요.", "en": "This book is fun too."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 ___ 가방을 원해요.", "answer": "작은", "options": ["작은", "작는", "작아요", "작다"], "en": "I want a small bag.", "why": "작다 has a consonant stem, so it takes 은 before a noun."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "그것은 ___ 차예요.", "answer": "비싼", "options": ["비싼", "비싸은", "비싸는", "비싸요"], "en": "That is an expensive car.", "why": "비싸다 has a vowel stem, so it takes ㄴ before a noun."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "이것은 ___ 길이에요.", "answer": "긴", "options": ["긴", "길은", "길는", "길어요"], "en": "This is a long street.", "why": "길다 drops its ㄹ and takes ㄴ, giving 긴."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "이것은 ___ 사과예요.", "answer": "맛있는", "options": ["맛있는", "맛있은", "맛있다", "맛있어요"], "en": "This is a delicious apple.", "why": "Adjectives ending in 있다 take 는 before a noun."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "___ 아이는 집에 있어요.", "answer": "행복한", "options": ["행복한", "행복하은", "행복하는", "행복해요"], "en": "The happy child is at home.", "why": "행복하다 has a vowel stem, so it becomes 행복한 before a noun."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "거리에 택시가 ___.", "answer": "많아요", "options": ["많아요", "많은", "많이에요", "많다요"], "en": "There are a lot of taxis on the street.", "why": "Noun이/가 많아요 says there are many of something."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저___ 영어를 배워요.", "answer": "도", "options": ["도", "는도", "도는", "을"], "en": "I learn English too.", "why": "도 replaces 는 entirely; it is never stacked with it."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "아버지는 사과___ 먹어요.", "answer": "도", "options": ["도", "를도", "도를", "은"], "en": "Father eats apples too.", "why": "도 replaces the object marker 를 instead of joining it."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "My wife is a happy person.", "ko": "제 아내는 행복한 사람이에요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "There are many people at the station.", "ko": "역에 사람이 많아요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "My son learns Korean too.", "ko": "제 아들도 한국어를 배워요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I live in a big house.", "ko": "저는 큰 집에 살아요."}], "reading": {"ko": "저는 지수예요. 저는 서울에 살아요. 서울은 아주 큰 도시예요. 거리에 사람이 많아요. 제 남편은 영어 선생님이에요. 저도 지금 영어를 배워요.", "en": "I am Jisu. I live in Seoul. Seoul is a very big city. There are a lot of people on the streets. My husband is an English teacher. I am learning English now too.", "questions": [{"q": "Where does Jisu live?", "a": "In Seoul (서울)."}, {"q": "What does her husband do?", "a": "He is an English teacher."}, {"q": "What is Jisu learning now?", "a": "English (영어)."}]}}}, {"n": 5, "title": "Lesson 5: Korean Conjugation: Past, Present, Future", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit1/unit-1-lessons-1-8/unit-1-lesson-5/", "vocab": [{"ko": "동생", "en": "younger sibling", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "남동생", "en": "younger brother", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "여동생", "en": "younger sister", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "형", "en": "older brother (said by a male)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "오빠", "en": "older brother (said by a female)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "누나", "en": "older sister (said by a male)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "언니", "en": "older sister (said by a female)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "삼촌", "en": "uncle", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "이모", "en": "aunt (on mother’s side)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "고모", "en": "aunt (on father’s side)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "아저씨", "en": "older man, mister", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "아주머니", "en": "older woman, ma'am", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "할아버지", "en": "grandfather", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "할머니", "en": "grandmother", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "친구", "en": "friend", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "사진", "en": "picture", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "안경", "en": "glasses", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "비밀", "en": "secret", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "비", "en": "rain", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "가게", "en": "store/shop", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "박물관", "en": "museum", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "가스 레인지", "en": "stove (gas range)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "오리", "en": "duck (animal)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "꼬리", "en": "tail", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "공", "en": "ball", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "기대하다", "en": "to expect (look forward to)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "건너다", "en": "to cross (a road/etc)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "던지다", "en": "to throw", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "싫어하다", "en": "to not like", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "떠나다", "en": "to leave somewhere", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "농담하다", "en": "to joke", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "공부하다", "en": "to study", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "지루하다", "en": "to be boring", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "마르다", "en": "to be thin (person)", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "멀다", "en": "to be far away", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "마르다", "en": "to be dry", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "비슷하다", "en": "to be similar", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "싫다", "en": "to not be good", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "오래되다", "en": "to be old (been around long)", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "배고프다", "en": "to be hungry", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "오늘", "en": "today", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "어제", "en": "yesterday", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "내일", "en": "tomorrow", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "모레", "en": "the day after tomorrow", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "년", "en": "year", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "일", "en": "day (with numbers: 3일)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "시간", "en": "time", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "월요일", "en": "Monday", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "화요일", "en": "Tuesday", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "수요일", "en": "Wednesday", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "목요일", "en": "Thursday", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "금요일", "en": "Friday", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "토요일", "en": "Saturday", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "일요일", "en": "Sunday", "pos": "noun"}], "grammar": [{"point": "Present tense (plain form) ~ㄴ/는다", "explanation": "Korean has a plain style used in writing: diaries, books, newspapers and essays. It isn't used when talking to people (it sounds blunt), but you will read it everywhere.\n\nFor verbs, look at the stem. If it ends in a vowel, add ㄴ다: 가다 → 가 + ㄴ다 → 간다; 공부하다 → 공부한다. If it ends in a consonant, add 는다: 먹다 → 먹 + 는다 → 먹는다; 읽다 → 읽는다. If it ends in ㄹ, drop the ㄹ and add ㄴ다: 살다 → 산다, 만들다 → 만든다.\n\nAdjectives don't change in the present; they stay in the dictionary form: 박물관은 멀다 (the museum is far). So 멀다 alone is a full sentence, but a verb needs its ending: 먹는다, not 먹다. 이다 also stays as it is: 나는 학생이다.", "examples": [{"ko": "나는 한국어를 공부한다.", "en": "I study Korean."}, {"ko": "동생은 밥을 먹는다.", "en": "My younger sibling eats rice."}, {"ko": "친구는 공을 던진다.", "en": "My friend throws the ball."}, {"ko": "그 박물관은 멀다.", "en": "That museum is far."}, {"ko": "삼촌은 한국에 산다.", "en": "My uncle lives in Korea.", "note": "살다 drops its ㄹ: 살 → 사 + ㄴ다 → 산다."}, {"ko": "오늘은 비가 온다.", "en": "It's raining today."}]}, {"point": "Past tense ~았/었/였다", "explanation": "To make the past tense, look at the last vowel of the stem. If it is ㅏ or ㅗ, add 았다: 만나다 → 만났다, 닫다 → 닫았다. For any other vowel, add 었다: 먹다 → 먹었다, 던지다 → 던졌다. 하다 becomes 했다: 공부하다 → 공부했다.\n\nWhen the stem ends in a vowel, the two vowels squeeze together. ㅏ + 았 → 았 (가다 → 갔다). ㅗ + 았 → 왔 (오다 → 왔다). ㅜ + 었 → 웠 (배우다 → 배웠다). ㅣ + 었 → 였 (던지다 → 던졌다). ㅓ + 었 → 었 (건너다 → 건넜다). ㅐ just gains ㅆ (끝내다 → 끝냈다).\n\nAdjectives work the same way: 비싸다 → 비쌌다 (was expensive). 이다 becomes 이었다 after a consonant and 였다 after a vowel: 학생이었다, 친구였다.\n\nThe same 았/었 is used in polite speech too (갔어요, 먹었어요), as Lesson 6 shows.", "examples": [{"ko": "나는 어제 가게에 갔다.", "en": "I went to the store yesterday."}, {"ko": "삼촌은 한국어를 공부했다.", "en": "My uncle studied Korean."}, {"ko": "동생은 공을 던졌다.", "en": "My younger sibling threw the ball."}, {"ko": "나는 친구를 만났다.", "en": "I met a friend."}, {"ko": "어제는 비가 왔다.", "en": "It rained yesterday.", "note": "오 + 았다 → 왔다."}, {"ko": "할아버지는 선생님이었다.", "en": "My grandfather was a teacher.", "note": "이다 after a consonant: 이었다."}]}, {"point": "Future tense ~겠다", "explanation": "~겠다 adds the idea of \"will\". It is the easiest ending to form: add 겠다 to the stem, whether it ends in a vowel or a consonant. 가다 → 가 + 겠다 → 가겠다; 읽다 → 읽 + 겠다 → 읽겠다; 공부하다 → 공부하겠다. There is no vowel matching and nothing merges.\n\nIt works best for your own intentions: 나는 내일 공부하겠다 (I will study tomorrow). It sounds firm, like a decision or a promise.\n\nIn polite speech it becomes 겠어요 or 겠습니다 (Lesson 6). You already know a fixed phrase with it: 잘 먹겠습니다, said before a meal (\"I will eat well\").\n\nKorean has another, more common future form that you will learn later. For now, use ~겠다 for \"I will\".", "examples": [{"ko": "나는 내일 공부하겠다.", "en": "I will study tomorrow."}, {"ko": "나는 박물관에 가겠다.", "en": "I will go to the museum."}, {"ko": "우리는 모레 떠나겠다.", "en": "We will leave the day after tomorrow."}, {"ko": "나는 오늘 책을 읽겠다.", "en": "I will read a book today."}, {"ko": "나는 모레 친구를 만나겠다.", "en": "I will meet a friend the day after tomorrow."}, {"ko": "나는 그 비밀을 말하겠다.", "en": "I will tell the secret."}]}, {"point": "Saying \"I\" and \"you\" (저/나, 너/당신)", "explanation": "Korean has two words for \"I\". 저 is polite: use it with anyone you speak politely to. 나 is casual: use it with close friends and younger people. With 이/가 they become 제가 and 내가.\n\n\"You\" is trickier, because Koreans usually avoid saying it. Instead they use the person's name + 씨, a title, or a family word: 민수 씨, 선생님 (teacher), 아저씨 (mister), 언니 (older sister). 민수 씨는 오늘 학교에 가요? means \"Are you going to school today, Minsu?\". Often \"you\" is simply left out: 학교에 가요?\n\n너 is the casual \"you\", only for close friends or people younger than you. With 이/가 it becomes 네가. In speech many people say 니가 so it doesn't sound like 내가.\n\n당신 appears in songs and between married couples, but said to a stranger it can sound rude. Avoid it.", "examples": [{"ko": "제가 의사예요.", "en": "I am the doctor."}, {"ko": "너는 내 친구야.", "en": "You are my friend."}, {"ko": "민수 씨는 오늘 학교에 가요?", "en": "Are you going to school today, Minsu?"}, {"ko": "네가 내 동생이야?", "en": "Are you my younger sibling?"}, {"ko": "오빠는 내일 떠나요?", "en": "Are you leaving tomorrow? (a woman to her older brother)", "note": "The family word 오빠 is used instead of \"you\"."}, {"ko": "너도 학생이야?", "en": "Are you a student too?"}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "동생은 사과를 ___.", "answer": "먹는다", "options": ["먹는다", "먹은다", "먹다", "먹어다"], "en": "My younger sibling eats an apple.", "why": "먹다 has a consonant stem, so the plain present is 먹는다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "형은 공을 ___.", "answer": "던진다", "options": ["던진다", "던지는다", "던지은다", "던지다"], "en": "My older brother throws the ball.", "why": "던지다 has a vowel stem, so the plain present adds ㄴ다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "그 가게는 ___.", "answer": "멀다", "options": ["멀다", "먼다", "멀는다", "멀어다"], "en": "That store is far.", "why": "Adjectives stay in their 다 form in the plain present."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "나는 어제 박물관에 ___.", "answer": "갔다", "options": ["갔다", "가았다", "간다", "가겠다"], "en": "I went to the museum yesterday.", "why": "가 + 았다 merges into 갔다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "언니는 어제 한국어를 ___.", "answer": "공부했다", "options": ["공부했다", "공부하았다", "공부한다", "공부하겠다"], "en": "My older sister studied Korean yesterday.", "why": "하 becomes 했다 in the past tense."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "우리는 어제 길을 ___.", "answer": "건넜다", "options": ["건넜다", "건너았다", "건넌다", "건너겠다"], "en": "We crossed the road yesterday.", "why": "건너 + 었다 merges into 건넜다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "나는 내일 가게에 ___.", "answer": "가겠다", "options": ["가겠다", "갔다", "가았겠다", "가겠았다"], "en": "I will go to the store tomorrow.", "why": "겠다 attaches straight to the stem to show your intention."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "___ 학생이야?", "answer": "네가", "options": ["네가", "너이", "너를", "너은"], "en": "Are you the student?", "why": "너 + 가 becomes 네가, just like 저 + 가 becomes 제가."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "My older brother (said by a man) learned Korean.", "ko": "형은 한국어를 배웠다."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I will meet my friend on Saturday.", "ko": "나는 토요일에 친구를 만나겠다."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "My younger sister dislikes rain.", "ko": "여동생은 비를 싫어한다."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Today is Monday.", "ko": "오늘은 월요일이다."}], "reading": {"ko": "어제는 토요일이었다. 나는 어제 박물관에 갔다. 박물관은 멀었다. 하지만 아주 재미있었다. 내 동생도 박물관을 좋아한다. 내일 나는 공부하겠다.", "en": "Yesterday was Saturday. I went to the museum yesterday. The museum was far. But it was a lot of fun. My younger sibling likes the museum too. Tomorrow I will study.", "questions": [{"q": "Where did the writer go yesterday?", "a": "To the museum (박물관)."}, {"q": "Was the museum close?", "a": "No, it was far."}, {"q": "What will the writer do tomorrow?", "a": "Study."}]}}}, {"n": 6, "title": "Lesson 6: Korean Honorifics", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit1/unit-1-lessons-1-8/unit-1-lesson-6/", "vocab": [{"ko": "신발", "en": "shoe", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "남방", "en": "shirt (casual button-up)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "질문", "en": "question", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "문제", "en": "question, problem", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "나이", "en": "age", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "화장실", "en": "bathroom, restroom", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "부장님", "en": "boss", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "분위기", "en": "atmosphere", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "차", "en": "tea", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "바지", "en": "pants", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "교실", "en": "classroom", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "급식", "en": "food at school", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "교감선생님", "en": "vice principal", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "교장선생님", "en": "principal", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "풀", "en": "glue", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "수도", "en": "capital city", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "병", "en": "bottle", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "병", "en": "disease, sickness", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "생선", "en": "fish (as food)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "야채", "en": "vegetable", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "언덕", "en": "hill", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "선물", "en": "present", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "기타", "en": "guitar", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "종이", "en": "paper", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "우유", "en": "milk", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "손목", "en": "wrist", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "시계", "en": "clock/watch", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "손목시계", "en": "wristwatch", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "영화", "en": "movie", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "노력하다", "en": "to try", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "앉다", "en": "to sit", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "만지다", "en": "to touch", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "자다", "en": "to sleep", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "보다", "en": "to see", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "기다리다", "en": "to wait", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "청소하다", "en": "to clean", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "약속하다", "en": "to promise", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "듣다", "en": "to hear", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "들어보다", "en": "to listen", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "그만하다", "en": "to stop (doing; quit)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "운동하다", "en": "to exercise", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "놀라다", "en": "to be surprised", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "빠르다", "en": "to be fast", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "느리다", "en": "to be slow", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "착하다", "en": "to be nice", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "곧", "en": "soon", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "항상", "en": "always", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "주", "en": "week", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "아래", "en": "bottom", "pos": "noun"}], "grammar": [{"point": "Informal low respect (반말) ~아/어", "explanation": "반말 is casual speech. Use it with close friends, people younger than you, and children. Using it with strangers or older people is rude.\n\nLook at the last vowel of the stem. If it is ㅏ or ㅗ, add 아: 앉다 → 앉 + 아 → 앉아. Otherwise add 어: 먹다 → 먹어. 하다 becomes 해: 청소하다 → 청소해.\n\nIf the stem ends in a vowel, things merge. ㅏ or ㅓ + 아/어 → nothing added (자다 → 자, 건너다 → 건너). ㅗ + 아 → ㅘ (보다 → 봐). ㅜ + 어 → ㅝ (배우다 → 배워). ㅣ + 어 → ㅕ (기다리다 → 기다려).\n\nPast is 았어/었어/했어 (봤어, 먹었어, 했어) and future is 겠어 (가겠어). 이다 becomes 야 after a vowel and 이야 after a consonant: 친구야, 학생이야. A question uses the same form with rising intonation: 자?", "examples": [{"ko": "나는 의자에 앉아.", "en": "I sit on the chair."}, {"ko": "동생은 지금 자.", "en": "My younger sibling is sleeping now."}, {"ko": "나는 어제 영화를 봤어.", "en": "I watched a movie yesterday."}, {"ko": "나는 교실을 청소했어.", "en": "I cleaned the classroom."}, {"ko": "나는 텔레비전을 봐.", "en": "I'm watching TV.", "note": "보 + 아 → 봐."}, {"ko": "너는 오늘 운동해?", "en": "Are you exercising today?"}]}, {"point": "Informal high respect ~아/어요", "explanation": "This is the polite style you will use most in Korea: with shop staff, coworkers, strangers and older people. It is polite without being stiff.\n\nIf you know 반말, just add 요: 앉아 → 앉아요; 봐 → 봐요; 기다려 → 기다려요; 해 → 해요. The vowel rule is the same: ㅏ/ㅗ → 아요, anything else → 어요, 하다 → 해요.\n\nPast is 았어요/었어요/했어요 (봤어요, 먹었어요, 청소했어요). Future is 겠어요 (청소하겠어요). 이다 becomes 이에요 after a consonant and 예요 after a vowel (never 야요).\n\nOne form can be a statement or a question, depending on your voice: 가요. (I'm going.) 가요? (Are you going?)", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 친구를 기다려요.", "en": "I am waiting for my friend."}, {"ko": "저는 어제 영화를 봤어요.", "en": "I watched a movie yesterday."}, {"ko": "저는 항상 운동해요.", "en": "I always exercise."}, {"ko": "저는 곧 청소하겠어요.", "en": "I will clean soon."}, {"ko": "그 선생님은 착해요.", "en": "That teacher is nice."}, {"ko": "버스를 기다렸어요?", "en": "Did you wait for the bus?", "note": "기다리 + 었어요 → 기다렸어요."}]}, {"point": "Formal high respect ~ㅂ니다/습니다", "explanation": "This is the most formal style. You hear it in the news, announcements, speeches, the army, from shop staff, and when speaking to a boss in a meeting. In daily conversation it can sound stiff, so ~요 is more common.\n\nIf the stem ends in a vowel, add ㅂ under the last syllable and then 니다: 기다리다 → 기다리 + ㅂ니다 → 기다립니다; 가다 → 갑니다; 하다 → 합니다. If it ends in a consonant, add 습니다: 앉다 → 앉습니다; 먹다 → 먹습니다.\n\nPast and future: 았/었 and 겠 end in a consonant, so they always take 습니다: 청소했습니다, 노력하겠습니다. 이다 becomes 입니다: 학생입니다.\n\nFor a question, change 다 to 까: 갑니까? (Are you going?), 먹습니까? (Do you eat?)", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 부장님을 기다립니다.", "en": "I am waiting for the boss."}, {"ko": "학생들은 교실에 앉습니다.", "en": "The students sit in the classroom."}, {"ko": "저는 어제 화장실을 청소했습니다.", "en": "I cleaned the bathroom yesterday."}, {"ko": "저는 열심히 노력하겠습니다.", "en": "I will try hard."}, {"ko": "우유를 좋아합니까?", "en": "Do you like milk?"}, {"ko": "이 기타는 제 선물입니다.", "en": "This guitar is my present.", "note": "이다 → 입니다."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 의자에 ___.", "answer": "앉아요", "options": ["앉아요", "앉어요", "앉요", "앉으요"], "en": "I sit on the chair.", "why": "The last vowel of 앉 is ㅏ, so it takes 아요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 친구를 ___.", "answer": "기다려요", "options": ["기다려요", "기다리요", "기다려어요", "기다립요"], "en": "I am waiting for my friend.", "why": "기다리 + 어요 merges into 기다려요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "동생은 지금 ___.", "answer": "자", "options": ["자", "자아", "자어", "잔요"], "en": "My younger sibling is sleeping now. (casual)", "why": "자 + 아 merges back into 자 in casual speech."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "나는 어제 영화를 ___.", "answer": "봤어", "options": ["봤어", "봐었어", "봤어다", "보어"], "en": "I watched a movie yesterday. (casual)", "why": "보 + 았어 merges into 봤어."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 항상 ___.", "answer": "운동해요", "options": ["운동해요", "운동하요", "운동하아요", "운동해어요"], "en": "I always exercise.", "why": "하 becomes 해, then 요 is added: 운동해요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 교실을 ___.", "answer": "청소합니다", "options": ["청소합니다", "청소하습니다", "청소해습니다", "청소햅니다"], "en": "I clean the classroom. (formal)", "why": "청소하 ends in a vowel, so it takes ㅂ니다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "아이는 의자에 ___.", "answer": "앉습니다", "options": ["앉습니다", "앉ㅂ니다", "앉읍니다", "앉아습니다"], "en": "The child sits on the chair. (formal)", "why": "앉 ends in a consonant, so it takes 습니다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 어제 친구를 ___.", "answer": "만났습니다", "options": ["만났습니다", "만나았습니다", "만났니다", "만납었니다"], "en": "I met my friend yesterday. (formal)", "why": "Past 만났 + 습니다 gives the formal past 만났습니다."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I am sitting on the sofa.", "ko": "저는 소파에 앉아요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I will exercise tomorrow. (formal)", "ko": "저는 내일 운동하겠습니다."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I touched the cat. (casual)", "ko": "나는 고양이를 만졌어."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I am waiting for the bus.", "ko": "저는 버스를 기다려요."}], "reading": {"ko": "저는 지금 교실에 있어요. 저는 의자에 앉아요. 저는 친구를 기다려요. 친구는 항상 느려요. 어제 우리는 영화를 봤어요. 영화는 아주 재미있었어요.", "en": "I am in the classroom now. I sit on a chair. I am waiting for my friend. My friend is always slow. Yesterday we watched a movie. The movie was very fun.", "questions": [{"q": "Where is the writer?", "a": "In the classroom (교실)."}, {"q": "Who is the writer waiting for?", "a": "A friend."}, {"q": "What did they do yesterday?", "a": "They watched a movie."}]}}}, {"n": 7, "title": "Lesson 7: Korean Irregulars", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit1/unit-1-lessons-1-8/unit-1-lesson-7/", "vocab": [{"ko": "눈썹", "en": "eyebrow", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "교사", "en": "teacher (job title)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "반", "en": "class (of students)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "직장", "en": "workplace", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "벽", "en": "wall", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "털", "en": "hair (not on head), fur", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "머리카락", "en": "hair (on head)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "저녁", "en": "dinner, evening", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "점심", "en": "lunch, noonish", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "옷", "en": "clothes", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "오전", "en": "morning (a.m.)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "오후", "en": "afternoon", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "여름", "en": "summer", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "가을", "en": "fall", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "겨울", "en": "winter", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "봄", "en": "spring", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "찾다", "en": "to search for, find", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "가르치다", "en": "to teach", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "일하다", "en": "to work", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "짓다", "en": "to build", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "가지다", "en": "to own, to possess", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "잠그다", "en": "to lock", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "잊다", "en": "to forget", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "돕다", "en": "to help", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "주다", "en": "to give", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "맞다", "en": "to be correct (right answer)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "쉽다", "en": "to be easy", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "덥다", "en": "to be hot (weather)", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "그립다", "en": "to miss (long for)", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "귀엽다", "en": "to be cute", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "춥다", "en": "to be cold (weather)", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "어렵다", "en": "to be difficult", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "더럽다", "en": "to be dirty", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "바쁘다", "en": "to be busy", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "같다", "en": "to be the same", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "안전하다", "en": "to be safe", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "딱딱하다", "en": "to be hard, to be rigid", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "부드럽다", "en": "to be soft", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "가능하다", "en": "to be possible", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "불가능하다", "en": "to be impossible", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "예쁘다", "en": "to be pretty", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "일찍", "en": "early", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "매일", "en": "everyday", "pos": "adverb"}], "grammar": [{"point": "ㅅ irregular", "explanation": "A few verbs whose stem ends in ㅅ lose the ㅅ when the next ending starts with a vowel (such as 아/어). The most common one is 짓다 (to build, to make, to give a name).\n\nStep by step: 짓다 → stem 짓 → before 어요 the ㅅ drops → 지 + 어요 → 지어요. Past: 지 + 었어요 → 지었어요. The vowels don't merge, so it stays 지어요, not 져요.\n\nBefore an ending that starts with a consonant, the ㅅ stays: 짓 + 습니다 → 짓습니다; 짓 + 겠어요 → 짓겠어요.\n\nNot every ㅅ verb is irregular. 웃다 (to laugh) and 씻다 (to wash) are regular: 웃어요, 씻어요. Learn the irregular ones as you meet them.", "examples": [{"ko": "아버지는 집을 지어요.", "en": "Father builds a house."}, {"ko": "그 회사는 큰 건물을 지었어요.", "en": "That company built a big building."}, {"ko": "저는 강아지 이름을 지었어요.", "en": "I named the puppy."}, {"ko": "그 회사는 매년 건물을 짓습니다.", "en": "That company builds buildings every year.", "note": "Before 습니다 the ㅅ stays."}, {"ko": "할머니는 밥을 지어요.", "en": "Grandmother cooks rice.", "note": "짓다 is also used for cooking rice: 밥을 짓다."}, {"ko": "저는 매일 웃어요.", "en": "I laugh every day.", "note": "웃다 is regular: 웃 + 어요 → 웃어요."}]}, {"point": "ㄷ irregular", "explanation": "Some verbs whose stem ends in ㄷ change the ㄷ to ㄹ when the next ending starts with a vowel. The common ones are 듣다 (to hear, to listen) and 걷다 (to walk).\n\nStep by step: 듣다 → stem 듣 → ㄷ becomes ㄹ → 들 + 어요 → 들어요. 걷다 → 걸 + 었어요 → 걸었어요.\n\nBefore an ending that starts with a consonant, the ㄷ stays: 듣 + 습니다 → 듣습니다; 걷 + 겠어요 → 걷겠어요.\n\nMany ㄷ verbs are regular, so you have to learn which is which: 닫다 → 닫아요, 받다 → 받아요.", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 음악을 들어요.", "en": "I listen to music."}, {"ko": "우리는 공원에서 걸었어요.", "en": "We walked in the park."}, {"ko": "저는 선생님의 질문을 들었어요.", "en": "I heard the teacher's question."}, {"ko": "저는 문을 닫았어요.", "en": "I closed the door."}, {"ko": "할아버지는 매일 걷습니다.", "en": "Grandfather walks every day.", "note": "Before 습니다 the ㄷ stays."}, {"ko": "저는 선물을 받았어요.", "en": "I received a present.", "note": "받다 is regular: 받 + 았어요 → 받았어요."}]}, {"point": "ㅂ irregular", "explanation": "Most adjectives whose stem ends in ㅂ, and a few verbs, change the ㅂ to 우 before an ending that starts with a vowel. The 우 then merges with 어 to make 워.\n\nStep by step: 춥다 → stem 춥 → ㅂ becomes 우 → 추우 + 어요 → 추워요. Likewise 쉽다 → 쉬워요, 덥다 → 더워요, 어렵다 → 어려워요. Past: 추웠어요. Before a noun (Lesson 4), 우 + ㄴ gives 운: 쉬운 책, 아름다운 여자.\n\n돕다 (to help) is special: the ㅂ becomes 오 instead, and 오 + 아 → 와, giving 도와요 and 도왔어요.\n\nBefore an ending that starts with a consonant, the ㅂ stays: 춥습니다, 덥겠어요. Some ㅂ verbs are regular, such as 잡다 (to grab): 잡아요.", "examples": [{"ko": "오늘은 너무 추워요.", "en": "It is too cold today."}, {"ko": "이 책은 쉬워요.", "en": "This book is easy."}, {"ko": "강아지가 정말 귀여워요.", "en": "The puppy is really cute."}, {"ko": "저는 어머니를 도와요.", "en": "I help my mother."}, {"ko": "어제는 정말 더웠어요.", "en": "It was really hot yesterday."}, {"ko": "저는 쉬운 책을 원해요.", "en": "I want an easy book.", "note": "쉽 → 쉬우 + ㄴ → 쉬운."}]}, {"point": "ㅡ irregular", "explanation": "When a stem ends in the vowel ㅡ (바쁘다, 예쁘다, 쓰다), the ㅡ disappears before 아/어. Then you add 아 or 어 to what is left.\n\nWhich one? Look at the vowel of the syllable before ㅡ. If it is ㅏ or ㅗ, use 아: 바쁘다 → 바빠요; 아프다 → 아파요; 고프다 → 고파요. Otherwise use 어: 예쁘다 → 예뻐요; 슬프다 → 슬퍼요.\n\nIf the stem has only one syllable, there is no earlier vowel, so use 어: 쓰다 → 써요; 크다 → 커요.\n\nPast works the same way: 바빴어요, 썼어요. Before an ending that starts with a consonant, nothing changes: 바쁩니다, 예쁜.", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 오늘 바빠요.", "en": "I am busy today."}, {"ko": "그 여자는 정말 예뻐요.", "en": "That woman is really pretty."}, {"ko": "저는 편지를 썼어요.", "en": "I wrote a letter."}, {"ko": "저는 배고파요.", "en": "I am hungry."}, {"ko": "저는 머리가 아파요.", "en": "I have a headache.", "note": "아프 + 아요 → 아파요."}, {"ko": "그 영화는 너무 슬펐어요.", "en": "That movie was so sad.", "note": "슬프 + 었어요 → 슬펐어요."}]}, {"point": "르 irregular", "explanation": "Stems ending in 르 (다르다, 빠르다, 모르다) change in a special way before 아/어. The ㅡ drops, an extra ㄹ is added to the syllable before, and the ending becomes 라 or 러.\n\nStep by step: 다르다 → stem 다르 → 다 gets an extra ㄹ → 달 + 라요 → 달라요. Likewise 빠르다 → 빨라요, 모르다 → 몰라요. Use 라 when the vowel before 르 is ㅏ or ㅗ, and 러 otherwise: 부르다 (to call) → 불러요.\n\nPast: 달랐어요, 빨랐어요, 몰랐어요.\n\nBefore an ending that starts with a consonant, nothing changes: 빠릅니다, 다른 (Lesson 4), and the very common phrase 모르겠어요 (I don't know).", "examples": [{"ko": "그 열차는 정말 빨라요.", "en": "That train is really fast."}, {"ko": "우리는 아주 달라요.", "en": "We are very different."}, {"ko": "저는 그 사람의 이름을 몰라요.", "en": "I don't know that person's name."}, {"ko": "그 남자는 아주 말랐어요.", "en": "That man is very thin.", "note": "마르다 → 말라; for being thin, Korean uses the past form."}, {"ko": "그 버스는 빠릅니다.", "en": "That bus is fast.", "note": "Before ㅂ니다 the stem doesn't change."}, {"ko": "저는 그 사람의 이름을 몰랐어요.", "en": "I didn't know that person's name."}]}, {"point": "ㄹ irregular", "explanation": "Stems ending in ㄹ (살다, 알다, 만들다, 길다, 열다) are mostly regular, but the ㄹ drops when the next part starts with ㄴ, ㅂ or ㅅ.\n\nIn the formal style, ㄹ stems take ㅂ니다 (not 습니다), and the ㄹ drops: 살다 → 사 + ㅂ니다 → 삽니다; 알다 → 압니다; 만들다 → 만듭니다. Before ㄴ it drops too: 길다 → 긴 (a long…), 살다 → 산다 (plain present).\n\nBefore an ending that starts with a vowel, the ㄹ stays and nothing unusual happens: 살아요, 알아요, 만들어요, 길어요. It also stays before 겠: 살겠어요.\n\nA quick check: if the next letter is ㄴ, ㅂ or ㅅ, drop the ㄹ; otherwise keep it.", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 한국에 삽니다.", "en": "I live in Korea."}, {"ko": "저는 그 사람을 압니다.", "en": "I know that person."}, {"ko": "아버지는 의자를 만듭니다.", "en": "Father makes chairs."}, {"ko": "저는 긴 편지를 썼어요.", "en": "I wrote a long letter."}, {"ko": "저는 그 선생님을 알아요.", "en": "I know that teacher.", "note": "Before a vowel the ㄹ stays: 알 + 아요 → 알아요."}, {"ko": "제 머리카락은 깁니다.", "en": "My hair is long.", "note": "길 → 기 + ㅂ니다 → 깁니다."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "삼촌은 큰 집을 ___.", "answer": "지었어요", "options": ["지었어요", "짓었어요", "짓어요", "지어었어요"], "en": "My uncle built a big house.", "why": "짓다 is ㅅ irregular, so the ㅅ drops before 었어요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 친구의 비밀을 ___.", "answer": "들었어요", "options": ["들었어요", "듣었어요", "들어었어요", "듯었어요"], "en": "I heard my friend's secret.", "why": "듣다 is ㄷ irregular, so ㄷ becomes ㄹ before a vowel."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 문을 ___.", "answer": "닫았어요", "options": ["닫았어요", "닫었어요", "다랐어요", "닫어요"], "en": "I closed the door.", "why": "닫다 is a regular ㄷ verb, so the ㄷ stays."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "겨울은 아주 ___.", "answer": "추워요", "options": ["추워요", "춥어요", "추와요", "춥아요"], "en": "Winter is very cold.", "why": "춥다 is ㅂ irregular: ㅂ becomes 우, which merges with 어요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 매일 친구를 ___.", "answer": "도와요", "options": ["도와요", "도워요", "돕아요", "돕어요"], "en": "I help my friend every day.", "why": "돕다 is the exception that becomes 도와요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "제 딸은 아주 ___.", "answer": "예뻐요", "options": ["예뻐요", "예쁘어요", "예빠요", "예뻬요"], "en": "My daughter is very pretty.", "why": "The ㅡ in 예쁘 drops, and 어요 is used because the vowel before it is not ㅏ or ㅗ."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "그 자전거는 아주 ___.", "answer": "빨라요", "options": ["빨라요", "빠르어요", "빨러요", "빠라요"], "en": "That bicycle is very fast.", "why": "빠르다 is 르 irregular: an extra ㄹ is added, giving 빨라요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 서울에 ___.", "answer": "삽니다", "options": ["삽니다", "살습니다", "살읍니다", "사습니다"], "en": "I live in Seoul. (formal)", "why": "살다 drops its ㄹ before ㅂ니다."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "This problem is easy.", "ko": "이 문제는 쉬워요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "The cat is soft.", "ko": "고양이는 부드러워요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I am busy in the afternoon.", "ko": "저는 오후에 바빠요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "My older sister (said by a woman) teaches English.", "ko": "제 언니는 영어를 가르쳐요."}], "reading": {"ko": "지금은 겨울이에요. 오늘은 너무 추워요. 저는 오늘 아주 바빠요. 저녁에 저는 친구를 도와요. 친구의 고양이는 아주 귀여워요. 고양이의 털은 부드러워요.", "en": "It is winter now. Today it is too cold. I am very busy today. In the evening I help my friend. My friend's cat is very cute. The cat's fur is soft.", "questions": [{"q": "What season is it?", "a": "Winter (겨울)."}, {"q": "What does the writer do in the evening?", "a": "Helps a friend."}, {"q": "What is the cat's fur like?", "a": "Soft."}]}}}, {"n": 8, "title": "Lesson 8: Korean Adverbs, Negative Sentences in Korean", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit1/unit-1-lessons-1-8/unit-1-lesson-8/", "vocab": [{"ko": "기계", "en": "machine", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "대학교", "en": "college, university", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "트럭", "en": "truck", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "검은색", "en": "black (color)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "흰색", "en": "white (color)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "음료수", "en": "beverage, drink", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "외국", "en": "foreign country", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "외국인", "en": "foreigner", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "고등학교", "en": "high school", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "도서관", "en": "library", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "곳", "en": "place", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "동시", "en": "same time", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "밤", "en": "night", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "어젯밤", "en": "last night", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "낮", "en": "daytime", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "동", "en": "East", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "남", "en": "South", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "서", "en": "West", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "북", "en": "North", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "놀다", "en": "to play", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "쓰다", "en": "to use", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "쓰다", "en": "to write", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "실수하다", "en": "to make a mistake", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "수리하다", "en": "to repair", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "잡다", "en": "to catch, to grab", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "읽다", "en": "to read", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "내다", "en": "to pay for", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "받다", "en": "to receive, to get", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "도착하다", "en": "to arrive", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "여행하다", "en": "to travel", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "완벽하다", "en": "to be perfect", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "아프다", "en": "to be sick, to be sore", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "똑똑하다", "en": "to be smart", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "중요하다", "en": "to be important", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "젊다", "en": "to be young (youthful adult)", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "늙다", "en": "to be old (aged: people/animals)", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "나쁘다", "en": "to be bad", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "바로", "en": "immediately (right away)", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "즉시", "en": "immediately (formal, instantly)", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "빨리", "en": "quickly/fast", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "자주", "en": "often", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "가끔", "en": "sometimes", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "많이", "en": "many/a lot of", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "방금", "en": "a moment ago", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "갑자기", "en": "suddenly", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "매년", "en": "every year", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "다시", "en": "again", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "혼자", "en": "alone", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "안", "en": "not", "pos": "adverb"}], "grammar": [{"point": "Adverbs of time, place and degree", "explanation": "Adverbs say when, where, how often or how something happens. Unlike nouns, they take no particle.\n\nTime words usually come early, often right after the subject: 저는 어제…, 저는 내일…. Some time nouns take 에 (어젯밤에, 아침에, 밤에), but 오늘, 어제, 내일 and 지금 never do.\n\nFrequency and manner adverbs (자주, 가끔, 매일, 빨리, 많이, 혼자, 다시) can come before the object or right before the verb: 저는 자주 도서관에 가요, 밥을 빨리 먹었어요. Both are natural.\n\nDegree words like 아주, 너무 and 정말 go right before the adjective or verb they strengthen: 아주 커요.", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 자주 도서관에 가요.", "en": "I often go to the library."}, {"ko": "저는 어젯밤에 혼자 놀았어요.", "en": "I played alone last night."}, {"ko": "저는 밥을 빨리 먹었어요.", "en": "I ate quickly."}, {"ko": "저는 가끔 친구를 만나요.", "en": "I sometimes meet my friend."}, {"ko": "갑자기 비가 왔어요.", "en": "Suddenly it rained."}, {"ko": "저는 방금 도착했어요.", "en": "I just arrived."}]}, {"point": "Adjective → adverb with ~게 / ~히", "explanation": "To say how something is done (\"easily\", \"safely\"), turn an adjective into an adverb.\n\nThe main way is to add 게 to the stem: 쉽다 → 쉽 + 게 → 쉽게 (easily); 행복하다 → 행복하게 (happily); 예쁘다 → 예쁘게 (prettily). It works for almost any adjective, and irregular stems don't change because 게 starts with a consonant.\n\nSome 하다 adjectives also have a 히 form: 안전하다 → 안전히, 조용하다 → 조용히 (quietly). Not all do (행복히 sounds odd), so 하게 is the safe choice.\n\nA few adverbs have their own form that you just learn: 많다 → 많이 (a lot), 빠르다 → 빨리 (quickly). The adverb usually goes right before the verb.", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 거리를 안전하게 건넜어요.", "en": "I crossed the street safely."}, {"ko": "우리는 행복하게 살아요.", "en": "We live happily."}, {"ko": "그 학생은 똑똑하게 말해요.", "en": "That student speaks smartly."}, {"ko": "저는 그 문제를 쉽게 끝냈어요.", "en": "I finished that problem easily."}, {"ko": "저는 문을 조용히 닫았어요.", "en": "I closed the door quietly."}, {"ko": "저는 밥을 많이 먹었어요.", "en": "I ate a lot.", "note": "많다 → 많이 (not 많게)."}]}, {"point": "Negation with 안", "explanation": "안 is the quickest way to make a sentence negative. Put it directly before the verb or adjective at the end: 먹어요 → 안 먹어요 (don't eat); 추워요 → 안 추워요 (isn't cold). Tense and politeness stay on the verb: 안 먹었어요, 안 가겠어요.\n\nFor noun + 하다 verbs, 안 goes between the noun and 하다: 공부하다 → 공부 안 해요; 운동하다 → 운동 안 해요. Saying 안 공부해요 is a common mistake. Adjectives with 하다 and verbs like 좋아하다 are one word, so 안 goes in front: 안 피곤해요, 안 좋아해요.\n\nDon't use 안 with 이다 or for \"not have\". Use 아니다 and 없다 instead (see below).\n\n안 is very natural in speech. ~지 않다 (next point) means the same thing.", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 생선을 안 먹어요.", "en": "I don't eat fish."}, {"ko": "오늘은 안 추워요.", "en": "It isn't cold today."}, {"ko": "저는 어제 공부 안 했어요.", "en": "I didn't study yesterday."}, {"ko": "동생은 학교에 안 가요.", "en": "My younger sibling doesn't go to school."}, {"ko": "저는 커피를 안 좋아해요.", "en": "I don't like coffee.", "note": "좋아하다 isn't noun + 하다, so 안 goes in front."}, {"ko": "어제는 안 바빴어요.", "en": "I wasn't busy yesterday."}]}, {"point": "Negation with ~지 않다", "explanation": "~지 않다 means the same as 안 but is built differently. Take the stem of the final verb or adjective, add 지, then add 않다 and conjugate 않다 for tense and politeness.\n\nStep by step: 먹다 → 먹 + 지 않다 → 먹지 않아요. 가다 → 가지 않아요. 어렵다 → 어렵지 않아요 (no irregular change, because 지 starts with a consonant). Past: 먹지 않았어요. Future: 가지 않겠어요. Formal: 먹지 않습니다.\n\nOnly 않다 changes; the main verb stays as a bare stem. Don't put the tense on both: 갔지 않았어요 is wrong.\n\nFor 하다 verbs, add 지 않다 to the whole stem: 공부하다 → 공부하지 않아요. ~지 않다 sounds a little more formal or written than 안.", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 생선을 먹지 않아요.", "en": "I don't eat fish."}, {"ko": "이 문제는 어렵지 않아요.", "en": "This problem isn't difficult."}, {"ko": "그 트럭은 빠르지 않아요.", "en": "That truck isn't fast."}, {"ko": "저는 내일 학교에 가지 않겠어요.", "en": "I won't go to school tomorrow."}, {"ko": "저는 어제 운동하지 않았어요.", "en": "I didn't exercise yesterday.", "note": "Past goes on 않다 only: 않 + 았어요."}, {"ko": "이 음료수는 비싸지 않습니다.", "en": "This drink isn't expensive."}]}, {"point": "아니다 (to not be)", "explanation": "아니다 is the negative of 이다: \"to not be\". Use it to say that A is not B, where B is a noun.\n\nThe pattern is A는/은 B이/가 아니에요. The noun before 아니다 takes 이/가: after a consonant 이 (선생님이 아니에요), after a vowel 가 (의사가 아니에요). Unlike 이다, 아니다 is a separate word, so leave a space.\n\nForms: polite 아니에요, casual 아니야, formal 아닙니다, past 아니었어요.\n\nDon't use 안 with 이다: 안 학생이에요 is wrong; say 학생이 아니에요. 아니요 (no) comes from the same word.", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 선생님이 아니에요.", "en": "I am not a teacher."}, {"ko": "이것은 제 가방이 아니에요.", "en": "This is not my bag."}, {"ko": "그 사람은 외국인이 아니에요.", "en": "That person is not a foreigner."}, {"ko": "나는 의사가 아니야.", "en": "I'm not a doctor."}, {"ko": "그 사람은 제 친구가 아니었어요.", "en": "That person was not my friend."}, {"ko": "여기는 도서관이 아닙니다.", "en": "This is not the library."}]}, {"point": "없다 (to not have / not be there)", "explanation": "없다 is the opposite of 있다 and covers both of its meanings. \"Not have\": Person는 Thing이/가 없어요 (저는 돈이 없어요). \"Not be there\": Thing이/가 Place에 없어요 (친구가 도서관에 없어요).\n\nIt conjugates regularly: 없 + 어요 → 없어요. Past: 없었어요. Formal: 없습니다. Casual: 없어.\n\nDon't say 안 있어요 for \"don't have\"; use 없어요. As with 있다, the thing takes 이/가, not 을/를: 시간이 없어요, not 시간을 없어요.\n\n없다 also appears inside other words: 맛없다 (to taste bad) and 재미없다 (to be boring) are the opposites of 맛있다 and 재미있다.", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 돈이 없어요.", "en": "I don't have money."}, {"ko": "우리는 시간이 없어요.", "en": "We don't have time."}, {"ko": "친구는 지금 도서관에 없어요.", "en": "My friend isn't at the library now."}, {"ko": "냉장고에 음료수가 없어요.", "en": "There are no drinks in the fridge."}, {"ko": "어제는 시간이 없었어요.", "en": "I didn't have time yesterday."}, {"ko": "이 영화는 재미없어요.", "en": "This movie is boring.", "note": "재미없다 is one word, the opposite of 재미있다."}]}, {"point": "싫어하다 vs 싫다", "explanation": "This pair works like 좋아하다 and 좋다 from Lesson 3. 싫어하다 is a verb meaning \"to dislike\". 싫다 is an adjective meaning \"to be unpleasant\".\n\nWith 싫어하다, a person dislikes something, so the thing takes 을/를: 저는 생선을 싫어해요. Conjugation: 싫어하 → 싫어해요.\n\nWith 싫다, the disliked thing is the subject and takes 이/가: 저는 비가 싫어요 (as for me, rain is unpleasant). Conjugation: 싫 + 어요 → 싫어요. The ㅎ is silent: it sounds like 시러요.\n\n싫다 describes your own feelings, so it is mostly used about yourself. For other people, 싫어하다 is more natural: 동생은 야채를 싫어해요. On its own, 싫어요! means \"No, I don't want to!\"", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 생선을 싫어해요.", "en": "I dislike fish."}, {"ko": "저는 비가 싫어요.", "en": "I don't like rain."}, {"ko": "동생은 야채를 싫어해요.", "en": "My younger sibling dislikes vegetables."}, {"ko": "저는 겨울이 싫어요.", "en": "I don't like winter."}, {"ko": "아버지는 고양이를 싫어하지 않아요.", "en": "Father doesn't dislike cats."}, {"ko": "야채를 싫어해요?", "en": "Do you dislike vegetables?"}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 야채를 ___ 먹어요.", "answer": "안", "options": ["안", "않", "아니", "없"], "en": "I don't eat vegetables.", "why": "안 goes directly before the verb to make it negative."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 어제 운동 ___ 했어요.", "answer": "안", "options": ["안", "않", "아니", "지"], "en": "I didn't exercise yesterday.", "why": "With noun + 하다 verbs, 안 goes between the noun and 하다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "그 책은 재미있지 ___.", "answer": "않아요", "options": ["않아요", "안아요", "않요", "아니에요"], "en": "That book isn't fun.", "why": "After ~지, use 않다, conjugated as 않아요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "이 도서관은 크___ 않아요.", "answer": "지", "options": ["지", "게", "고", "기"], "en": "This library isn't big.", "why": "The negative pattern is stem + 지 않다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 외국인___ 아니에요.", "answer": "이", "options": ["이", "가", "을", "를"], "en": "I am not a foreigner.", "why": "The noun before 아니다 takes 이/가, and 외국인 ends in a consonant."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "냉장고에 우유___ 없어요.", "answer": "가", "options": ["가", "를", "이", "에"], "en": "There is no milk in the fridge.", "why": "The missing thing takes 이/가 with 없다, and 우유 ends in a vowel."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 겨울___ 싫어요.", "answer": "이", "options": ["이", "을", "가", "를"], "en": "I don't like winter.", "why": "싫다 is an adjective, so the disliked thing takes 이/가, not 을/를."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "아이는 ___ 놀아요.", "answer": "행복하게", "options": ["행복하게", "행복한", "행복해", "행복하지"], "en": "The child plays happily.", "why": "Adjective stem + 게 makes an adverb."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I don't have a car.", "ko": "저는 차가 없어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I sometimes travel alone.", "ko": "저는 가끔 혼자 여행해요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "This is not my book.", "ko": "이것은 제 책이 아니에요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "My younger brother dislikes fish.", "ko": "제 남동생은 생선을 싫어해요."}], "reading": {"ko": "저는 학생이에요. 저는 자주 도서관에 가요. 하지만 어제는 도서관에 안 갔어요. 저는 많이 아팠어요. 오늘은 아프지 않아요. 지금 저는 다시 도서관에 가요.", "en": "I am a student. I often go to the library. But yesterday I didn't go to the library. I was very sick. Today I'm not sick. Now I am going to the library again.", "questions": [{"q": "How often does the writer usually go to the library?", "a": "Often (자주)."}, {"q": "Why didn't the writer go yesterday?", "a": "They were very sick."}, {"q": "Where is the writer going now?", "a": "To the library again."}]}}}]);
